<!--
  <ship-app-chrome> — @ship/chrome · the per-app root layout wrapper.

  A chrome wrapper for a route LAYOUT (routes/_layout.sui → prefix '/',
  the longest-prefix match covering the index route AND every nested
  route). Pairs with the bundler's root-layout discovery
  (`crates/deck/deck-bundler/src/routes.rs::discover_layouts`); see the
  job spec at `.artifacts/job-spec.md` (job/deck-root-layout) for the
  chain.

  Composes:
    <ship-nav-top>  the filled top bar (brand, project switcher,
                    breadcrumbs, theme picker, account menu, etc.)
    <slot name="route" />  the route content — the runtime's
                            `mountRouter` (packages/deck/route/src/
                            mount-router.ts::buildMountTarget) inserts
                            a <div slot="route"> here via the chrome
                            chain, and `mount()` projects the route
                            view into that div through this named slot.

  Why a wrapper, not just `<ship-nav-top>` in the layout file? The
  `extract_layout_tag` codegen reads the FIRST `<sui-*` / `<ship-*`
  element of the `_layout.sui` and emits it as the LAYOUTS `tag`; the
  runtime then `document.createElement(tag)`s that element. The
  wrapper IS the chrome. A naked `<ship-nav-top>` would mount the bar
  but never receive the route content — the bar has no
  `<slot name="route">`, by design (it composes the slotless
  `<sui-nav-top>` primitive). This wrapper stitches the two: the bar
  PLUS the named slot the router fills.

  Layout: `position: fixed; inset: 0` so the chrome fills the viewport
  exactly (bypasses the browser's default 8px body margin) and is
  immune to content-driven growth. Inner column flex: the bar is
  fixed-height; the main region takes the remaining height via `flex:1`.

  Slots: `route` (router fills this) and `command-bar` (forwarded
  through to `<ship-nav-top slot="command-bar">`). Any consuming
  layout that wants a trigger pill in the top bar slots its
  `<sui-command-bar slot="command-bar">` into this wrapper and the
  forwarding slot chain carries it through the shadow boundaries:
  `_layout` → `ship-app-chrome` → `ship-nav-top` → `.cmd-slot`.
  Other view-injected top-bar content (actions, pickers) belongs on
  `<ship-nav-top>` directly — not on this wrapper — to avoid
  duplicating the slot surface.
-->

<style>
  :host {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--background);
    color: var(--foreground);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
  }
  :host([hidden]) { display: none; }

  .app-main {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    overflow: auto;
  }
</style>

<ship-nav-top>
  <slot name="command-bar" slot="command-bar"></slot>
</ship-nav-top>
<main class="app-main" part="main">
  <slot name="route"></slot>
</main>
